Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Convert the markdown text to HTML formatted text
    std::string html = "";
    std::istringstream stream(markdown);
    std::string line;

    while (std::getline(stream, line)) {
        html += line;
    }

    return html;
}
```

This function takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. The function uses a `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line and appends each line to the `html` string.

Here's an example usage of the function:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello, World!\nThis is a paragraph.\nIt's some more text.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This will output:

```
<h1>Hello, World!</h1>This is a paragraph. It's some more text.
```

